WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.629
Robot heads, dangerous blood, and…Bing Bong?

00:00:03.629 --> 00:00:07.466
All these references in the first episode
of "She-Hulk" mean something big.

00:00:07.466 --> 00:00:11.270
Viewers who are otherwise unfamiliar with
She-Hulk may be surprised when Jennifer Walters

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:13.150
looks directly at the camera and says,

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:15.639
"It's true, I am a Hulk."

00:00:15.639 --> 00:00:19.619
Longtime Marvel Comics fans, however, will
happily recognize She-Hulk breaking the fourth

00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:21.859
wall to acknowledge she's a fictional character.

00:00:21.859 --> 00:00:26.339
As head writer Jessica Gao revealed to Total
Film, the fourth wall-demolishing series "Fleabag"

00:00:26.339 --> 00:00:29.590
is a major influence on "She-Hulk: Attorney
at Law."

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:33.660
But Jennifer Walters has been having metafictional
adventures since John Byrne's "Sensational

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:36.400
She-Hulk" #1 hit the stands in 1989.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:41.079
Gao told Variety that Jennifer's rule-breaking
irreverence is rooted in Byrne's reinvention

00:00:41.079 --> 00:00:42.220
of the character.

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:46.450
The episode's opening scene sets the tone
for the series with even more comic book references.

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:51.510
Nikki Ramos, Jennifer's paralegal and best
friend, suggests she show a savage look, a

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:55.379
nod to Jennifer's very first Marvel Comics
series, "Savage She-Hulk."

00:00:55.379 --> 00:00:59.270
Jennifer's rehearsed closing argument for
an upcoming case also includes the phrase,

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:03.899
"Those with the most power have the most to
answer for."

00:01:03.899 --> 00:01:06.500
That sounds quite similar to Spider-Man's
motto,

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.600
"With great power…"

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.435
"...comes great responsibility."

00:01:11.435 --> 00:01:14.160
A flashback reveals She-Hulk's harrowing origin.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.050
While on vacation, Jennifer and her cousin
Bruce Banner encounter a spaceship from the

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:22.670
planet Sakaar — where Hulk found fame as
a gladiator in "Thor: Ragnarok" — which

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:24.640
forces their car off a cliff.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.610
Jennifer pulls Bruce from the wreckage, inadvertently
mixing his blood with hers and triggering

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:32.560
her first transformation into She-Hulk.

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:38.140
This scene is a dramatic update of She-Hulk's
origin as first depicted in 1980's "Savage

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:39.220
She-Hulk" #1.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:44.030
There, Bruce gives Jennifer an emergency blood
transfusion after she's shot by a mobster.

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:48.530
The danger of anyone potentially coming into
contact with Bruce's blood is established

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:52.100
in 2008's "The Incredible Hulk," in which
Bruce is hiding in Brazil and working in a

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.680
bottling factory.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:58.000
Blood from his cut finger contaminates a bottle
of soda, which then gives an elderly man,

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.260
played by Stan Lee, gamma sickness.

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:04.600
So Jennifer's inadvertent blood transfusion
is a callback to a significant moment in Bruce's

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:07.990
early history and a modernization of her classic
comic book origin.

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:12.420
This time, Jennifer becomes She-Hulk because
she risks her life to save Bruce, giving her

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.305
more agency and control over her story.

00:02:15.305 --> 00:02:19.450
After her first night as She-Hulk, Jennifer
wakes up in an isolated beach house in Mexico

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:21.710
where Bruce has taken her to recover.

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:26.650
Walking around the house, she sees a battered
gray and gold head belonging to an Iron Legion droid.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:31.990
Developed by Tony Stark, the Iron Legion appears
in 2015's "Avengers: Age of Ultron" as a peacekeeping

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:35.520
force that aids the Avengers on an early mission
to Sokovia.

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:39.730
After Tony and Bruce design the advanced A.I.
system Ultron, it gains self-awareness and

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:42.370
activates inside an Iron Legion droid.

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:46.610
Ultron leads the rest of the automated army
to attack the unprepared Avengers.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:50.220
In the basement laboratory of the Mexico beach
house, Bruce explains to Jennifer that the

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:53.960
house was a gift from the now-deceased Tony
Stark, and that they spent time here in the

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:55.550
years following the Blip.

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:59.230
Tony's influence is visible throughout the
house, especially in the Stark tech used in

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:01.760
the lab and the initials he carved into the
bar.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:03.120
"Tony mostly drank.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.630
Sat around, complaining about Steve."

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:06.640
"Oh, Steve."

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:13.016
The Iron Legion helmet is a bittersweet reminder
of the two Avengers' combined hopes and failures.

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:17.270
"She-Hulk: Attorney at Law" is adept at dropping
pop culture references, including nods to

00:03:17.270 --> 00:03:18.870
other Disney properties.

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:22.230
In the premiere, Smart Hulk explains that
Jennifer's transformations are triggered by

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:23.550
intense emotion.

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:28.110
In an effort to gauge her transformational
threshold, he traps her in a booth with buzzing sawblades.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:29.151
But Jennifer has a better idea:

00:03:29.151 --> 00:03:31.760
"Put on a Pixar movie.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:34.610
When Bing Bong jumps out of the wagon in 'Inside
Out.'"

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:35.610
"No!

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:36.610
Oh!

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:37.610
Bing Bong!"

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:38.610
Pixar likely needs no introduction.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:41.310
The animation studio has produced some of
the most widely beloved and tear-jerking family

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:43.240
films of the last three decades.

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:47.440
This is a sly bit of corporate synergy — Pixar
and Marvel are both owned by Disney — but

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:49.010
it also means much more.

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:53.140
"Inside Out," specifically, stars Riley, a
young girl struggling with a move.

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:58.440
She's steered through life by the personified
emotions of joy, sadness, disgust, fear, and anger.

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:02.629
One of the movie's biggest themes is the danger
of allowing one emotion to dominate the others

00:04:02.629 --> 00:04:06.920
— something of particular relevance to the
Hulks, whose transformations are triggered

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:08.230
by fear and anger.

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:12.120
The scene with Bing Bong, Riley's imaginary
friend, that Jennifer mentions symbolizes

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:14.310
the loss of childhood innocence.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:18.299
Like Riley, Jennifer is adjusting to a significant
change in her life — but where She-Hulk

00:04:18.299 --> 00:04:21.394
is concerned, the growing pains are very literal.

00:04:21.394 --> 00:04:25.449
The first episode of "She Hulk" features a
confrontation between cousins Jennifer and

00:04:25.449 --> 00:04:28.010
Bruce guaranteed to leave Marvel fans smiling.

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:31.779
Not only does the audience get to see the
two jade giants square off for the first time,

00:04:31.779 --> 00:04:37.249
Bruce unleashes a classic Hulk move that hasn't
been seen on-screen since 2008's "The Incredible Hulk."

00:04:37.249 --> 00:04:40.409
"For the record, I really didn't want to do
this."

00:04:40.409 --> 00:04:44.600
At this point, Bruce slaps his palms together,
creating a blast of wind powerful enough to

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:46.150
send her flying.

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:50.639
This is Hulk's famous thunderclap attack,
first used to devastating effect in 1962's

00:04:50.639 --> 00:04:52.560
"Incredible Hulk" #4.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:56.529
It creates a shockwave powerful enough to
strike down enemies, level cities, and even,

00:04:56.529 --> 00:05:03.210
in 2011's "Incredible Hulks" #634, put the
formidable dragon Fin Fang Foom out to pasture.

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:05.610
But the thunderclap can save people, too.

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:09.279
In the movie "The Incredible Hulk," he claps
to extinguish the flames engulfing a downed

00:05:09.279 --> 00:05:12.660
helicopter, saving Betty Ross and Thunderbolt
Ross.

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:16.430
After picking herself up, She-Hulk tries her
hand at the thunderclap, with less-than-perfect

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:20.889
results. She soon gets the hang of it, allowing her
to show Bruce what his signature move feels

00:05:20.889 --> 00:05:22.643
like on the receiving end.

